  "# closing: fleet cleanup 2026-08-12

One `git-cleanup-conflict` pass over the superproject and its west children.
Everything below was measured, not inferred; where a check could not run, that
is stated rather than assumed.

## Landed on default branches

| PR | What | State |
|---|---|---|
| com-junkawasaki/root#2092 | ISIC-9601 camera flags (item 3/7) | merged |
| com-junkawasaki/root#2106 | board capture replayed with camera preserved | merged (by the cursor agent, after #2091 was flagged) |
| cloud-itonami/loop-noren#1 | retarget `commoncrawl-actor` `:local/root` to its west path | merged |
| net-kotobase/commoncrawl-actor#2 | `:dev` alias siblings -> `../../kotoba-lang/<name>` | merged |
| kotoba-lang/loop-innen#4 | 11 rescued resident-tick commits (append-only ledger + corpus) | merged |

Two west pins advanced through `scripts/west-pin-put.cljs` (server-side
reachability + no-regression + blob precondition): `commoncrawl-actor`
64cbdc7d -> 9e549701, `loop-noren` 6a10ab6e -> c13dc288.

## Decision 1 — two agents wrote mutually invalidating PRs; do not merge both

root#2091 and #2092 were opened 32 seconds apart by parallel agents that never
saw each other. #2091 replaced `bin/render.cljs` wholesale
(`@@ -1,360 +1,50 @@`); #2092 patched that same file in six places, adding
`--orbit/--zoom/--fov/--eye/--target`. Both were independently MERGEABLE against
main, so nothing in GitHub's own signals showed the collision.

Verified before acting: the rewritten `render.cljs` in #2091 contained **no**
camera flags, and its `render_street.cljs` only read `[:globals :eye]` for a HUD
label. Taking #2091's side of a conflict resolution would therefore have deleted
camera control that had just landed.

#2092 merged first (item 3/7 precedes 4/7; its delta is additive where the other
is a rewrite). #2091 was commented with the exact incompatibility rather than
rebased or hand-resolved. Its author replayed onto current main as #2106,
preserving the flags in `render_street.cljs`, and closed #2091. Confirmed on
main: `accept_camera.cljs`, `render_board.cljs`, and 6 orbit references in
`render_street.cljs` all present.

**Rule reinforced**: two PRs each reporting MERGEABLE against main can still be
mutually exclusive. Mergeability is pairwise-with-main, never transitive.

## Decision 2 — a repo transfer broke deps twice, and only the second break was visible

`west-orphan-audit --blocking` reported exactly one blocking `:local/root` edge
fleet-wide: `orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase-commoncrawl-actor`, `dir=true west=false`,
1 consumer. The cause was a transfer to `net-kotobase/commoncrawl-actor`, already
registered in west at `orgs/net-kotobase/commoncrawl-actor`. The local tree only
kept working because a leftover checkout of the pre-transfer path was still on
disk; a fresh checkout would not resolve.

Materializing the repo at its registered west path exposed **six further broken
edges**: its own `:dev` alias still resolved siblings as `../<name>`, which now
points into `orgs/net-kotobase/` where none of `langgraph`, `langchain-store`,
`langchain`, `org-chainagnostic-cacao`, `org-ietf-ed25519`, `org-ietf-cbor` live.
The alias comment stated its own precondition — *\"once this repo lives at
orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase-commoncrawl-actor\"* — which stopped holding at
transfer time.

**Rule**: a repo transfer invalidates relative `:local/root` paths in BOTH
directions — consumers pointing at the old path, and the repo's own aliases
pointing at siblings by relative depth. Audit both. The second class is invisible
until the repo is checked out at its new path, so materializing it is part of the
diagnosis, not an afterthought.

Containment verified before retiring the leftover: old HEAD `5616bc5` is an
ancestor of the successor's main (`behind_by: 0`); only `README.md` differed.
Result: `local-root-broken: 0`.

## Decision 3 — a rate-limited write path invented parentless commits

`cleanup-land.cljs --apply` was run while GitHub's **secondary** rate limit was
active (every request 403 while `rate_limit` still reported ~4,880/5,000 core
remaining — the two are different buckets and the core counter does not reveal
the secondary block).

The script's write path is blob -> tree(base_tree) -> commit -> ref. With
`base_tree` unreadable it did not fail closed: it created commits with
`parents: 0`. Six repos received branches with *\"No common ancestor\"* to their
default branch, whose trees held only the new files and none of the repo's
history — unmergeable by construction and invisible unless compared against the
default branch.

**Defect to fix in `scripts/cleanup-land.cljs`**: a failed base-tree read must
abort the repo, never fall through to a root commit. This is the same class as
the `:jq-scalar-is-not-json` trap already recorded — a failed lookup silently
reinterpreted as a legitimate value.

The six refs were archived (sha + mode + blob-sha + path per file) and deleted.

## Decision 4 — the additive backlog was already landed

Re-running with a working API, the idempotency check found **28 of 31 untracked
files byte-identical to what is already on main** (org-bitcoin-p2p 8, app-hyakka
16, org-threejs 2, local-murakumo 2). The remaining 3 were untracked locally but
present-and-different on main, so the script correctly demoted them to `:review`.

**Correction of record**: an earlier statement in this session framed that
`:additive` class as work at risk of loss. It was not — it was residue from
earlier landings, exactly the re-run case `:land` documents. The survey's
`untracked=N` marker means *not committed in this working tree*, which is not the
same as *not present on main*.

## Decision 5 — the cleanest merge candidate in the backlog was the one that had to be blocked

`kotoba-lang/bonsai#16` measured `+29/-0`, one file, MERGEABLE, base at tip, zero
overlap, zero deletions — every numeric rule said merge. The file is
`gen-shadow-cljs-edn.bb`, beginning `#!/usr/bin/env bb`.

ADR-2607173000 retired babashka as a script host and CLAUDE.md forbids new
`bb.edn` / `#!/usr/bin/env bb`. Merging would have reintroduced a forbidden
runtime entry point through a cleanup PR whose diff looked harmless.

**Rule**: the disposition ladder (drift / overlap / deletions) is necessary but
not sufficient. A policy read of the content is required before `:merge`. The
script's idea — deriving `shadow-cljs.edn` `:source-paths` from `clojure -Spath`
rather than a hand-duplicated list — is sound and should be ported to nbb.

## Retirement performed

17 worktrees -> 4-5 (fluctuating; other sessions create their own), 19 local
branches -> 8-9, 0 stashes of mine. Every branch archived to
`.git/stash-archive-20260812/` (8.2M) before deletion, per the non-negotiable
`:retirement :archive` rule. A 31M nested west checkout under `root-native-wx`
was removed only after confirming all 11 child repo HEADs reachable upstream and
its one substantive local edit (amu deps + test) superseded by upstream main.

## Deliberately not done

- **25 un-landed ledger events** (`fleet-db.ledger.edn`) across 3 branches plus 5
  uncommitted in the `kotobase-pins-v2` worktree. Append-only signed VCS plane;
  hand-editing forbidden. Events archived verbatim with provenance to
  `.git/stash-archive-20260812/unlanded-ledger-events-20260812/`; branches kept.
  Belongs to fleet reconcile, not cleanup.
- **31 PRs in archived repos** (`gftdcojp/241001-lifescience-web` 29,
  `kotoba-lang/kotoba-v2025` 2). Read-only: close returns 403 and GitHub refuses
  to re-run workflows older than a month. Requires owner unarchive -> close ->
  re-archive. Must be counted separately or the backlog never appears to shrink.
- **~690 `kotoba-lang/com-*` repos showing `dirty=7`**: committed `.cpcache/`
  build output, locally deleted. Deletions are never replayed. This single
  artifact is 689 of the survey's 1,081 UNLANDED rows — the real residual is 392.
  `.cpcache` is tracked and absent from `.gitignore` in those repos; removing it
  fleet-wide is a decision, not cleanup debris.
- **46 dependabot PRs** — out of cleanup scope.

## Verification actually run

- `nbb scripts/west-orphan-audit.cljs --blocking` -> `local-root-broken: 0`, exit 0
- `nbb scripts/cleanup.cljs --unlanded` -> full fleet survey, 4,141 repos, completed
- `nbb scripts/west-pin-put.cljs` -> server-side verified both pin advances
- conflict-marker grep over tracked superproject files -> clean
- EDN reader check on the rewritten `commoncrawl-actor/deps.edn` before commit
- content-containment for every retired branch and for the path-override leftover

Not run: repo test suites. Nothing in this session changed executable logic
except two `deps.edn` path retargets, whose correctness is structural (the target
paths exist under west) rather than behavioural.

## Open at close — a second instance of Decision 2, in flight

Re-running the audit at close time returned `local-root-broken: 1` again, on a
NEW edge: `orgs/net-kotobase/kotobase-object-store-b2-s3`, `dir=false west=false`,
1 consumer. Diagnosed, deliberately not fixed.

`orgs/net-kotobase/engine` was created at 19:20 today by another live session
(tip: *Merge pull request #1 from network-awai/codex/transfer-to-net-kotobase*),
part of an ongoing split of the `orgs/network-awai/net-kotobase` monorepo into
per-package repos under a `net-kotobase` org — `authn`, `commoncrawler`, `cypher`,
`docs`, `engine`, `ipfs`, `lake` are already through, all west-registered.

`engine`'s `deps.edn:171` carries `{:local/root "../kotobase-object-store-b2-s3"}`.
That was correct while `engine` was a directory inside the monorepo and the
target was its sibling directory. Now that `engine` is its own repo, the relative
path resolves to `orgs/net-kotobase/kotobase-object-store-b2-s3`, which does not
exist: `kotobase-object-store-b2-s3` is **still a subdirectory** of
`orgs/network-awai/net-kotobase` and has not been split out. It is not a repo of
its own, so it cannot simply be registered — it has to be extracted first, or the
dep retargeted at the monorepo path.

Not repaired here because the migration is still running; editing those
`deps.edn` files now would race the session performing it. This is the same
failure Decision 2 describes, which means that rule now has predictive value
rather than only retrospective: **every repo split must audit relative
`:local/root` depth in both directions before the split is called done.**

## Resume point

0. `orgs/net-kotobase/engine` -> `kotobase-object-store-b2-s3` (above). Owner of
   the net-kotobase split should either extract that package into its own repo
   and register it, or retarget the dep. Re-check with
   `nbb scripts/west-orphan-audit.cljs --blocking`.
1. ~~Fix the `parents: 0` fallback in `scripts/cleanup-land.cljs`~~ **DONE**
   (root#2128, merged 2026-08-12). `gh-ref` now returns
   `{:state :found/:absent/:error}` and treats only `(HTTP 404)` as absent;
   `server-commit!` aborts the repo on `:error`, and also when `base-sha`
   resolved but its tree did not — the same trap one call later, which would
   have written a tree containing none of the repo's existing files. The
   branch-existence check had the identical collapsed test, which is what
   produced the run's `Reference already exists (HTTP 422)` errors.
   Verified by fault injection (a `gh` shim failing only `git/ref/heads/*`
   with 403, everything else passing through): the run aborts with the
   fail-closed message and the target repo's branch list is unchanged. The
   `404 -> root commit` path for genuinely empty placeholder repos is
   unchanged by inspection, not by test — exercising it would require faking a
   404 against a repo that has commits, i.e. manufacturing the exact artifact
   the fix prevents.
2. Work the 26 dispositioned preservation PRs — each carries its own
   measurements and recommended verdict as a comment. 12 are
   `close-stale-revert-risk`, 14 `needs-review`.
3. A `:close-*` disposition does not settle a PR: `cleanup-land` never deletes
   local WIP, so unless the shared checkout's working tree is committed, ignored,
   or reconciled with main, the next run recreates the same PR. Every comment
   says so, otherwise a resurrected PR gets counted as fresh backlog.
4. Owner-only: unarchive the two archived repos to clear 31 stuck PRs.
"}]
